{"title":"Copy of Test 2","sections":[{"id":0,"heading":"Preliminary","number":1,"category":"","questions":[{"qid":"question-1kssobnpu","title":"Are you a data controller or processor processing personal data to which the GDPR applies?","number":1,"description":"","additional_details":"GDPR applies to you if you are processing personal data in any of the following ways:

(1) Processing personal data of data subjects who are in the EU, where the processing activities are related to the monitoring of the behavior of data subjects as far as their behavior takes place within the EU.

(2) Processing personal data of data subjects who are in the EU, where the processing activities are related to the offering of goods or services, to data subjects in the EU.

(3) Processing personal data in the context of the activities of an establishment in the EU, regardless of whether the processing takes place in the EU or not.

(4) Processing personal data in a place where member state law applies by virtue of public international law when you are not established in the EU.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":[],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":null,"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssofx70","title":"Have you mapped all your data flows of personal data?","number":2,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB has mandated all data exporters to undertake a mapping exercise of their data flows. You must know exactly to which jurisdictions your data is being transferred. You must take into account all onward transfers (transfers from non-EEA processors to other non-EEA processors - in the same third country or another) as well as access or storage in a cloud outside of EEA. You must document this process for future auditing purposes.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Very High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf you do not know to which jurisdictions your data is being sent, you are in violation of the EDPB guidelines on international data transfers. This is because certain jurisdictions provide inadequate protection to personal data and have surveillance laws and practices that are disproportional and unnecessary. The protections provided by your Article 46 transfer tool might not be adequate to protect the data from such illegal interference, thus you might need to undertake supplementary measures to protect the transferred personal data.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eYou are advised to conduct a comprehensive mapping exercise of your data flows immediately and document the results.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssok2m8","title":"Are you transferring data to a recipient jurisdiction outside the EEA which does not have an adequacy decision by the EU Commission?","number":3,"description":"","additional_details":"EDPB has mandated additional responsibilities for data exporters and importers for transfers of personal data between the EEA to a jurisdiction that does not have an adequacy decision. This is because countries without an adequacy decision could have surveillance laws or practices which allow public authorities of the jurisdiction unnecessary, disproportionate and overbroad access to the transferred personal data, risking the rights and freedoms of EU residents. Thus these transfers might require supplementary measures for the protection of the personal data from the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":[],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":null,"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssokx19","title":"If you are transferring personal data outside the EEA, are these cross-border data transfers limited to what are necessary and adequate?","number":4,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB requires that all data transfers should be analyzed and only minimal and necessary personal data should be transferred which is adequate for the purposes of the processing. This analysis also requires that there are no suitable alternatives to the transfer. Document this analysis for future auditing purposes.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eBy transferring more data than the absolute minimum required, you are violating the EDPB guidelines on international data transfers. By sending personal data of data subjects beyond what is necessary and adequate to jurisdictions outside of the EEA that do not have an adequacy decision (and most probably provide inadequate protections), you are opening yourself to reputational, regulatory, and investment risk.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eBased on your response, you need to reduce your cross-border data transfers to only those which are essential and necessary and reduce the amount of personal data transferred to what is minimal and required for the purposes of the processing.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":4,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssorc6s","title":"State the recipient jurisdiction you are filling out this assessment for?","number":5,"description":"","additional_details":"Please state only one recipient jurisdiction in the text box below. If there are other recipient jurisdictions, you should complete this assessment and begin another for every recipient jurisdiction.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Content","hideLabel":false,"optionsLabelPosition":"","values":null,"customClass":"","tableView":false,"type":"textarea","key":"textPlain","input":true,"defaultValue":null,"multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":null,"responseTypes":["textPlain"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":null,"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":5,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null}],"data_map_metadata":null,"edit_permissions":{"OWNER":false,"USER":false,"COLLABORATOR":false}},{"id":0,"heading":"Derogations","number":2,"category":"","questions":[{"qid":"question-1kssp543e","title":"Are you undertaking these transfers utilizing the limited exceptions/derogations provided for under Article 49 of the GDPR?","number":1,"description":"","additional_details":"If you are conducting transfers of personal data covered under the GDPR to non-adequate jurisdictions without the use of Article 46 transfer tools, you must ensure that these transfers fall within the limited exceptions/derogations provided for under Article 49 of the GDPR. These exceptions/derogations can only be relied upon for occasional transfers of personal data and in exceptional cases. They are as follows:

*The transfer has been conducted upon the explicit consent of the Data Subject;
*The transfer is necessary for the performance of a contract (or pre-contractual measures) between the Data Subject and Data Controller;
*The transfer is necessary for the performance of a contract between a legal guardian of the Data Subject and Data Controller, for the benefit of the Data Subject;
*The transfer is necessary for important reasons of public interest;
*The transfer is necessary for the establishment or exercise of legal claims or defenses;
*The transfer is necessary for the vital interests of the data subject - and the data subject is unable to consent;
*The transfer is made from a register which according to Union or Member State law is intended to provide information to the public;
*The transfer is necessary for the purposes of compelling legitimate interests pursued by the controller which are not overridden by the interests or rights and freedoms of the data subject - provided no other exception/derogation applied and the Supervisory Authority and the Data Subject are notified of the transfer.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Multiple-Choice","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"Yes, but only for some transfers","value":"yesButOnlyForSomeTransfers","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-compliance-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"multipleChoice","input":true,"defaultValue":null,"multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":[],"responseTypes":["multipleChoice"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":null,"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksspvb6g","title":"Which derogation under Article 49 are you relying upon?","number":2,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB has provided separate guidances on the limitations on transfers under each derogation under Article 49. Please select which derogation you are relying upon.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Multi-Select","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Transfer with the consent of the data subject","value":"Choice 1","shortcut":""},{"label":"Transfer for fulfillment of contract in interest of or upon the request of the data subject","value":"Choice 2","shortcut":""},{"label":"Transfer to pursue an important public Interest","value":"Choice 3","shortcut":""},{"label":"Transfer for establishment, defense or exercise of legal claim","value":"Choice 4","shortcut":""},{"label":"Transfer for the protection of vital interests of data subject","value":"transferForTheProtectionOfVitalInterestsOfDataSubject","shortcut":""},{"label":"Transfer in pursuit of a compelling legitimate interest","value":"transferInPursuitOfACompellingLegitimateInterest","shortcut":""},{"label":"Other","value":"other","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-multi-selectbox","tableView":true,"type":"selectboxes","key":"multiSelect","input":true,"defaultValue":{"Choice 1":false,"Choice 2":false,"Choice 3":false,"Choice 4":false},"multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":[],"responseTypes":["multiSelect"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":null,"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksspxs93","title":"If you are relying on the consent derogation, are you collecting data subjects' consent in accordance with EDPB requirements?","number":3,"description":"","additional_details":"Consent is an important derogation. You must ensure that you properly document the collection of data subjects' consent for future audits. You must also remember that as per the EDPB, consent-based transfers can only be occasional, exceptional, and strictly necessary. Finally, the consent has to be collected correctly for it to be valid, as per the EDPB's issued guidelines on 4th May 2020, consent is only valid if it is:

*Freely given: it was provided without coercion and on a completely voluntary basis and the data subject is allowed to withdraw consent at any time and without any detriment;
*Specific: it is limited for the purpose it was asked for and not for other uses of the data;
*Informed: the data subject was made aware of all the risks and consequences of their decision to provide it;
*Unambiguous: it has to be provided explicitly by a clear act of the data subject - ambiguous manners of the collection are not allowed.

While transferring any special categories of data, you must ensure that the data subject has provided explicit consent. The term "explicit" refers to an express statement of consent, preferably in writing. In the digital context, explicit consent may be provided in the form of an electronic signature, by sending an email, by uploading a scanned document, or any other similar mechanism ensuring express and explicit consent of the data subject.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eNot collecting data subjects consent in accordance with EDPB requirements (consent has to be freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous) and relying on this derogation for international data transfers is a violation of the GDPR and opens you up to regulatory risk (fines from the Supervisory Authority which can go up to 4% of global revenues) and data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you should review your consent collection techniques and bring them in line with EDPB requirements and document your consent collection comprehensively for future data audits by Supervisory Authorities before continuing to transfer data subjects\u0026#39; data across borders.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":3,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksspzmy6","title":"If you are relying on the 'contractual necessity' derogation, is this data transfer necessary and limited as per EDPB requirements to fulfill a contract between you and the data subject?","number":4,"description":"","additional_details":"Contractual necessity is an important derogation. However, necessity requires a higher threshold than just convenience for business purposes. The necessity must arise from an objective, close and substantial connection between the data transfer and the purposes of the contract. A good rule of thumb is to consider whether the contract will be frustrated if the transfer does not take place. It’s also important to remember only occasional transfers are allowed. Transfers arising from stable business relationships which lead to repetitive transfers cannot be justified under this derogation. It would be wise to review all transfers under this derogation and note if they are occasional or repetitive and record and document reasons for the necessity for future data audits.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eNot ensuring that all the EDPB requirements and conditions are met when transferring data internationally relying on this derogation is a violation of the GDPR and opens you up to regulatory risk (fines from the Supervisory Authority which can go up to 4% of global revenues) and data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you should review all of your data transfers relying on this derogation to ensure they are necessary, limited, and follow all the EDPB requirements and conditions for the reliance on this derogation before continuing to transfer data subjects\u0026#39; data across borders.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":4,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssq2esx","title":"If you are relying on "important public interest" derogation, is this data transfer necessary and limited as per EDPB requirements in pursuit of achieving an important public interest?","number":5,"description":"","additional_details":"In order to rely on the ‘important public interest’ exception for data transfers, you should be aware that as per the EDPB guidelines, such transfers are only applicable if the Union or Member State laws you are subject to recognize the public interest. It is not sufficient for such laws to only exist though. It must be apparent from the law or policy of the Union or Member State being relied upon that such transfers are possible for important public interest reasons including ‘spirit of reciprocity and ‘international cooperation'. Transfers under such derogations must be occasional and not systematic and any transfers made should be strictly limited and well documented, with all justifying material for the transfer being available for audit purposes.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eNot ensuring that all the EDPB requirements and conditions are met when transferring data internationally relying on this derogation is a violation of the GDPR and opens you up to regulatory risk (fines from the Supervisory Authority which can go up to 4% of global revenues) and data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you should review all of your data transfers relying on this derogation to ensure they are necessary, limited, and follow all the EDPB requirements and conditions for the reliance on this derogation before continuing to transfer data subjects\u0026#39; data across borders.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":5,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssq58ek","title":"If you are relying on "establishment, exercise or defence of a legal claim" derogation, is this data transfer necessary and limited as per EDPB requirements in pursuit of establishing, exercising, or defense of a legal claim?","number":6,"description":"","additional_details":"In order to rely on ‘establishment, defense or exercise of legal claims’ derogation, you must ensure that data transfers are occasional and necessary in relation to a contract or a legal claim. A close and substantial link between the data transferred and a specific procedure regarding the situation in question regardless of whether in a judicial proceeding or whether in an administrative or any out-of-court procedure (including procedures before regulatory bodies) is necessary. The mere interest of third-country authorities or possible “good will” to be obtained from the third country authority as such would not be sufficient nor would mere possibility that legal proceedings may be brought in the future. For such transfers it is recommended to document that the data being transferred is justified by the necessity for the purposes of establishing, defending, or executing a legal claim (or to ensure an out-of-court settlement to a proceeding). A legal opinion from your lawyers on the necessity of the transferred data for these purposes should suffice for documentation purposes.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eNot ensuring that all the EDPB requirements and conditions are met when transferring data internationally relying on this derogation is a violation of the GDPR and opens you up to regulatory risk (fines from the Supervisory Authority which can go up to 4% of global revenues) and data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you should review all of your data transfers relying on this derogation to ensure they are necessary, limited, and follow all the EDPB requirements and conditions for the reliance on this derogation before continuing to transfer data subjects\u0026#39; data across borders.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":6,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssq8tqd","title":"If you are relying on "protection of vital interest of the data subject" derogation, is this data transfer necessary and limited as per EDPB requirements for protecting the vital interests of the data subject, provided that the data subject is physically or legally incapacitated to give consent?","number":7,"description":"","additional_details":"To make transfers ‘for the data subject’s vital interests’ when they do not possess the 'mental or legal capacity' you must ensure that the data subject has a medical emergency or any other such situation which reaches the required threshold where the imminent risk of serious harm to the data subject outweighs data protection concerns. You must also ensure the data being sent is necessary to reduce or mitigate the risk of serious harm to the data subject i.e if it is a medical emergency only data required to treat the data subject can be sent. Lastly, it is important to ensure the data subject is mentally or legally incapacitated to give consent. If they are not, then consent needs to be taken regardless of the risk of harm. If you are transferring data under this derogation you must document all the reasons and justifications diligently along with all supporting evidence (especially related to the data subject’s physical or mental incapacity which might require a medical or legal opinion from a relevant professional) for data audit purposes.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eNot ensuring that all the EDPB requirements and conditions are met when transferring data internationally relying on this derogation is a violation of the GDPR and opens you up to regulatory risk (fines from the Supervisory Authority which can go up to 4% of global revenues) and data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you should review all of your data transfers relying on this derogation to ensure they are necessary, limited, and follow all the EDPB requirements and conditions for the reliance on this derogation before continuing to transfer data subjects\u0026#39; data across borders.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":7,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssqajyh","title":"If you are relying on "compelling legitimate interest" derogation, is the data transfer necessary and limited as per EDPB requirements for the purposes of pursuing a compelling legitimate interest?","number":8,"description":"","additional_details":"For transfers of data made by you for pursuing ‘compelling legitimate interests’ it is necessary that you take into account all factors, especially the risk of harm to the data subject, and balance them with the legitimate interests you are pursuing. The important factors that need to be accounted for by you in this balancing exercise to take advantage of this derogation are:

*The EDPB sees this as a measure of last resort. It must be shown by you that none of the safeguards were a viable option for this data transfer and none of the other derogations could be reasonably relied upon to make this data transfer;
*Particular consideration to the nature of the personal data, the purpose and duration of the proposed processing operation or operations, as well as the situation in the country of origin, the third country, and the country of final destination needs to be taken by you; 
*A higher threshold is required as your legitimate interests need to be ‘compelling’ i.e they must be requiring urgent attention or there might be a high risk of loss or harm to you (personal, financial, reputational, etc.);
*Transfers under this derogation cannot be repetitive - they must be exceptional;
*Transfers cannot be on a large scale i.e transfer must be limited to the data of just those data subjects whose data needs to be transferred necessarily;
*The data subject and relevant data protection authority need to be informed of the transfer. 
*Any risks to the data subjects found in the balancing exercise conducted by you must be mandatorily addressed by employing additional safeguards to mitigate the risk of harm to the data subject.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eNot ensuring that all the EDPB requirements and conditions are met when transferring data internationally relying on this derogation is a violation of the GDPR and opens you up to regulatory risk (fines from the Supervisory Authority which can go up to 4% of global revenues) and data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you should review all of your data transfers relying on this derogation to ensure they are necessary, limited, and follow all the EDPB requirements and conditions for the reliance on this derogation before continuing to transfer data subjects\u0026#39; data across borders.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":8,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null}],"data_map_metadata":null,"edit_permissions":{"OWNER":false,"USER":false,"COLLABORATOR":false}},{"id":0,"heading":"Transfer Tools","number":3,"category":"","questions":[{"qid":"question-1kssqgzdq","title":"Are you utilizing Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs), Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs), or any other transfer tool under Article 46 of the GDPR to conduct the transfer of data?","number":1,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB requires all Data Exporters to audit their data flows and the Article 46 transfer tool being relied upon to provide the necessary appropriate safeguards to the transferred data especially if an Article 49 derogation does not apply to the transferred data. Article 46 safeguards can be summarized as the following: 

*A legally binding and enforceable instrument between public authorities or bodies;
*Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs);
*Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs);
*Standard data protection clauses adopted by a supervisory authority and approved by the Commission;
*Certification under an approved certification mechanism together with binding and enforceable commitments of the receiver outside the EEA;
*Contractual clauses authorized by a supervisory authority;
*Administrative arrangements between public authorities or bodies authorized by a supervisory authority.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk","Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["yes"],"risk":{"level":"Low","description":"\u003cp\u003eThe appropriate safeguards provided by Article 46 of the GDPR are effective tools to provide protections to covered personal data, however with the CJEU\u0026#39;s recent judgement in C-311/18 (Schrems II), there are new obligations for all data exporters and importers need to undertake to ensure the transferred personal data remains pro\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eYou should continue this assessment and undertake a legal analysis of the recipient jurisdiction\u0026#39;s legal system and assess whether the appropriate safeguards you have applied on the transferred personal data via Article 46 tool will be legally enforceable and remain effective or not.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}},{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are not utilizing a transfer tool under Article 46 of the GDPR to transfer data to non-adequate jurisdictions outside the EEA and these transfers are not covered under any of the limited exceptions under Article 49 derogations, you are in clear violation of the GDPR and can be subject to strict penalties, going up to 4% of the global revenue of your company.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring any data which is not covered by the Article 49 derogations, you must ensure you apply the protections of an Article 46 transfer tool to those transfers. Consider implementing Article 46 transfer tools to your data transfers or immediately suspend all transfers to non-adequate jurisdictions outside the EEA and have data importers delete (including backups) or return all previously transferred data immediately.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":1,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null}],"data_map_metadata":null,"edit_permissions":{"OWNER":false,"USER":false,"COLLABORATOR":false}},{"id":0,"heading":"Impact Assessment","number":4,"category":"","questions":[{"qid":"question-1kssqqipe","title":"Have you undertaken a legal assessment of the recipient jurisdiction to assess the effectiveness of the safeguards by Article 46 transfer tool to protect the transferred personal data?","number":1,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB requires that data exporters, with the aid of data importers, analyze the laws/regulations/practices of the recipient jurisdiction to assess the effectiveness of the protections provided to the transferred data by the Article 46 transfer tool. It is expected that data exporters must ensure that Article 46 tools are not just paper protections to the transferred data - they should be enforceable and practically implementable protections in the recipient jurisdiction. The standard for the protection of the transferred data has to be 'essentially equivalent to the protections provided to the personal data in the EU (through the GDPR). Focus on laws and practices which would apply to the transferred data, the data importer, and would impinge the effectiveness of the safeguards contained in the Article 46 transfer tool in particular. You should document the process. It is important to note that the scope of your assessment is limited to the legislation and practices relevant to the protection of the specific data you transfer. 

The following things need to be considered to conduct a holistic assessment:

*Scale and regularity of transfers;
*Length of the processing chain;
*Number of actors involved and the transmission channels used; 
*Purposes for which the data are transferred and processed;
*Types of entities involved in the processing; 
*Sector/Industry in which the transfer occurs (and any sector-specific laws/regulations/practices);
*Categories of personal data transferred; 
*Whether the data will be stored in the recipient jurisdiction or whether there is only remote access to data stored within the EU/EEA;
*Format of the data to be transferred; 
*Possibility that the data may be subject to onward transfers to another jurisdiction;
*Past experiences transferring to the recipient jurisdiction (whether any data access requests by public authorities of the recipient country have ever been received);
*Rule of law situation in the recipient jurisdiction;
*Existence of a comprehensive data protection law or an independent data protection authority (or sectoral specific if applicable) in the recipient jurisdiction;
*Adherence to international instruments providing for data protection safeguards by the recipient jurisdiction.
* If the data importer’s commitments enabling DSR requests as provided in the Article 46 GDPR transfer tool (such as access, correction, and deletion requests for transferred data, as well as (judicial) redress) can be effectively applied in practice under the law of the recipient jurisdiction.
*Sources of information on the basis of which you assess the law and practice of the recipient jurisdiction.

You must ensure the information you rely on is relevant, objective, reliable, verifiable, and accessible.  You can even take into account the experience of other actors operating within the same sector and/or related to similar transferred personal data and documented practical experience of the importer with
relevant prior instances of requests for access received from public authorities in the third country as an additional source of information. For more information on which sources you can rely on, you should refer to paragraphs 44-47 and Annex 3 of the EDPB's latest recommendations on supplementary safeguards for cross-border transfer.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eThe EDPB in its most recent guidance on international transfers has mandated all data exporters to undertake a legal assessment of the recipient jurisdiction if personal data covered by the GDPR is being transferred to a non-EEA jurisdiction that does not have an adequacy decision from the EU Commission. If this analysis is not undertaken, it risks opening you up to regulatory risks as the relevant Supervisory Authorities may ask for a record of this assessment and its result during a future audit and may order the suspension of the data transfer and impose fines if it is discovered you have no taken this activity as different \u0026#39;inadequate\u0026#39; non-EEA jurisdictions may have laws and practices which nullify the protections provided to the transferred personal data by the Article 46 tool - a fact that will not be discovered unless this legal analysis is undertaken.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003ePlease conduct a comprehensive legal analysis of the recipient jurisdiction to identify any gaps in the legal system which undermine the protections provided by the Article 46 transfer tool utilized to provide an \u0026#39;essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection to the transferred personal data. Document this process and its results.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":1,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssqw8mo","title":"Have you analyzed the surveillance laws and practices of the recipient jurisdiction (i.e laws that allow surveillance/access/acquisition of data by public authorities) in the legal analysis mentioned above?","number":2,"description":"","additional_details":"EDPB requires that data exporters must focus particularly on surveillance laws and practices of recipient jurisdictions which allow public authorities in the recipient jurisdiction to surveil/access/acquire the transferred personal data. For the purposes of these laws, data exporters must focus on EDPB's guidance on EU essential guarantees to see whether these laws and practices conform with these guarantees. If these essential guarantees are not respected by the laws and practices allowing public authorities to surveil the transferred data, then this interference has to be considered unjustifiable and a violation of the safeguards provided to the personal data by the Article 46 transfer tool. It is important that the primary focus of the analysis should be surveillance laws (and other laws and regulations providing public authorities similar powers in relation to the transferred personal data). However, in case there are no laws on public surveillance in the recipient jurisdiction (or they are not publically available), other objective factors can be relied upon such as known practices, reported precedents, legislation, established practices as well as technical, financial, and human resources of the recipient jurisdiction's public authorities in being able to intercept communications of the transferred data and being able to overcome technical protections (i.e encryption, etc.) It is also important to note that while the laws of the recipient jurisdiction might guarantee EU essential guarantees, the practices of public authorities may clearly indicate that they do not normally apply/comply with the legislation that governs their activities. The following factors must be focused upon as per the EDPB guidance on EU essential guarantees:

*Legal basis: Interference/surveillance must be based on clear, precise, foreseeable, and accessible rules contained in legally binding domestic law in the recipient country.

*Necessity and proportionality: Interference/surveillance must be strictly necessary and proportional to the objectives/aims pursued by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction (the interference/surveillance must not be general or bulk in nature and must define the scope of the interference).

*Independent oversight mechanisms: There must be an independent and impartial mechanism (such as a judge) to oversee the implementation of surveillance measures in the third country to reduce arbitrariness and abuse.

*Effective remedies for data subjects: There must be effective remedies available within the law for a data subject to exercise their rights and seek redress when public authorities do not comply with the domestic laws or rules on public surveillance - this is linked with the right of data subject to be notified of the interference. An independent authority such as a judicial authority must be entrusted with listening to Data Subjects' redressal requests and must have the power to remedy non-compliance by public authorities and enforce data subjects' rights.

*Analysis of practices of public authorities, if there are no surveillance laws or rules (or publicly available laws or rules) or the laws, are not followed by the public authorities in practice.

Significant consideration must be made to supplementary measures where an assessment results that the third country does not meet the above-mentioned minimum Essential Guarantees.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eThe EDPB in its most recent guidance on international transfers has mandated all data exporters to undertake a legal assessment of the recipient jurisdiction if personal data covered by the GDPR is being transferred to a non-EEA jurisdiction which does not have an adequacy decision from the EU Commission - focusing especially on public surveillance laws and practices providing public authorities access to the transferred personal data and the conformity of these laws and practices in ensuring the effectiveness of the Article 46 tool safeguards, the essentially equivalent protection of the transferred personal data and the respect of the EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees. If this analysis is not undertaken, it risks opening you up to regulatory risks as the relevant Supervisory Authorities may ask for a record of this assessment and its result during a future audit and may order the suspension of the data transfer and impose fines if it is discovered you have no taken this activity as different \u0026#39;inadequate\u0026#39; non-EEA jurisdictions may have laws and practices which nullify the protections provided to the transferred personal data by the Article 46 tool - a fact that will not be discovered unless this legal analysis is undertaken.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003ePlease conduct a comprehensive legal analysis of the recipient jurisdiction to identify surveillance laws and practices that are unjustifiable and undermine the protections provided by the Article 46 transfer tool utilized to provide an \u0026#39;essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection to the transferred personal data and the EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU Essential Guarantees. Document this process and its results.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":2,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssr0uvx","title":"Does the recipient jurisdiction provide an essentially equivalent level of protection to the transferred data and make the safeguards employed for the protection of the transferred data under the Article 46 tool implementable and effective?","number":3,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB requires that data exporters with the assistance of data importers assess the laws and practices of the recipient jurisdiction so as to confirm if the appropriate safeguards for the transferred data under the Article 46 transfer tool are implementable and effective measures and thus the level of protection for the transferred data is 'essentially equivalent'. This process should be documented and if any gaps are identified, they should be addressed with 'supplementary measures'. It should be noted:

*The appropriate safeguards contained in the Article 46 tool must be implementable and effective (i.e if the law of the recipient jurisdiction prohibits a practice or enforceability of the SCCs against the data importer, then those protections must be deemed unimplementable and ineffective);

*Since SCCs and BCRs (and most of the other Article 46 transfer tools) are private agreements (i.e contractual guarantees) between two private parties, surveillance laws or practices providing public authorities access to the transferred data can overcome the safeguards contained therein;

*If surveillance laws or practices in the recipient country that provide public authorities powers to surveil/access/acquire the transferred data are not violative of the EDPB's guidance on EU essential guarantees, they are not violative of the 'essentially equivalent' level of protection provided to the data by the safeguards contained in the Article 46 transfer tool;

*However, if the surveillance laws and practices of the recipient country are violative of EDPB's guidance on EU essential guarantees, then the protections afforded to the transferred personal data by the Article 46 transfer tools might be unimplementable or ineffective;","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf the recipient jurisdiction\u0026#39;s laws or practices make the safeguards for the transferred personal data contained in the Article 46 transfer tool unimplementable or ineffective or the surveillance laws or practices that authorize public authorities to surveil/access/acquire the transferred personal data do not meet the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees, then the transferred personal data is no longer provided an \u0026#39;essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection and thus data subject\u0026#39;s rights under the GDPR are threatened and at risk. If this risk goes unaddressed it opens you up to regulatory risk (fines up to 4% of global revenues and bans on further transfers). Thus it is important to address this risk by employing \u0026#39;supplementary measures\u0026#39;.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is important that you undertake \u0026#39;supplementary measures\u0026#39; to further protect the personal data to overcome the gaps recognized in your legal assessment of the recipient jurisdiction\u0026#39;s legal system and surveillance practices.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":3,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null}],"data_map_metadata":null,"edit_permissions":{"OWNER":false,"USER":false,"COLLABORATOR":false}},{"id":0,"heading":"Supplementary Measures","number":5,"category":"","questions":[{"qid":"question-1kssr5t1g","title":"Are you taking or can you take supplementary measures to protect the transferred personal data and ensure it receives an 'essentially equivalent' level of protection in the recipient jurisdiction?","number":1,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB in its most recent guidance on international data transfers has recommended data exporters to ensure supplementary measures (technical measures supplemented by contractual and organizational measures) are implemented when the legal analysis of a non-EEA recipient jurisdiction (with no adequacy decision) reveals that the safeguards for the data transferred under the Article 46 tool are unimplementable or ineffective especially if this is due to surveillance laws or practices, which provide the recipient jurisdiction's public authorities the power to surveil/access/acquire the transferred personal data in excess of the standards established by EDPB's guidance on EU essential guarantees. However, you may decide to proceed with the transfer without being required to implement supplementary measures, if you consider that you have no reason to believe that relevant and problematic legislation identified by your assessment will be applied, in practice, to your transferred data and/or importer. It is important to note that to take advantage of this exemption, you must prepare a detailed report to show that the guarantees provided by the Article 46 transfer tool shall not be impugned by the problematic legislation since they do not apply in practice to the transferred data or importer. However, the report does not exempt you from providing for the necessary supplementary measures (e.g. end-to-end encryption of data) to protect personal data during its transmission and processing in the recipient jurisdiction if your analysis of the applicable legislation of the recipient jurisdiction indicates that access to data may also take place, even in the absence of the importer’s intervention at this moment of transfer.

Each supplementary measure should also be assessed under which conditions it would be effective in covering the gaps or deficiencies in the legal setup of the recipient jurisdiction which threaten the transferred data. It should be carefully examined whether the supplementary measures can be taken (are there any laws which prohibit the implementation or enforcement of the supplementary measures), whether they shall be effective (do they address the gap or deficiency completely and in the different and various ways the data can be surveilled/accessed/acquired by public authorities) and whether the data importer shall implement them. Some of the data planned for transfer may require supplementary measures whereas other data may not (considering formal and/or practical application of the recipient jurisdiction's law). Factors companies must account for in implementing supplementary measures are the following (non-exhaustive):

*Format of the data to be transferred (i.e. in plain text/pseudonymized or encrypted); 
*Nature of the data (e.g. a higher level of protection is afforded in the EEA to categories of data covered by articles 9 and 10 GDPR);
*Length and complexity of data processing workflow;
*Number of actors involved in the processing, and their relationship;
*Possibility that the data may be subject to onward transfers, within the same or even to another third country.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf the recipient jurisdiction does not have an adequacy decision and transfers are based on Article 46 transfer tools and the legal analysis of the recipient jurisdiction reveals deficiencies that would make the Article 46 safeguards unimplementable or unenforceable especially against public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction which can surveil/access/acquire the transferred personal data in excess of the standards established by the EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees, the deficiencies and gaps must be covered by implementing supplementary measures (technical measures supplemented by contractual and organizational measures). These measures must directly address the gaps and deficiencies uncovered. If supplementary measures are not implemented it will risk affected data subjects\u0026#39; personal data to illegal access/surveillance and not allow them to exercise their rights enshrined under the Article 46 transfer tool which was used to make the transfer, which is a violation of the EDPB guidance and shall make you liable for breach of the GDPR and thus result in regulatory risks (fines amounting to 4% of global revenues and a ban on further international transfers) during audit of data processing activities as well as to data subjects\u0026#39; private rights of actions. If supplementary measures cannot be employed, the transfers should immediately seize and all data transferred previously must be deleted or returned by the data importer or you must show through a detailed report that the problematic legislation in the recipient jurisdiction identified in your assessment, which impugns the effectiveness of the Article 46 transfer tools, will not be applied in practice to your transferred data and/or importer.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eImmediately implement supplementary measures to address the deficiencies and gaps identified in the legal analysis of the recipient jurisdiction to continue data transfers or prepare a detailed report to show the problematic legislation in the recipient jurisdiction identified by your assessment shall not apply in practice to the transferred data or data importer. If you cannot comply with these requirements, it is recommended that you have all personal data previously transferred deleted or returned by the data importer and suspend all present or future data transfers unless supplementary measures are taken (or can be taken) or a report can be prepared to show the transferred data shall not be affected by the problematic legislations identified in your assessment.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssr9khy","title":"If you cannot or are not implementing supplementary measures for the affected recipient jurisdictions, have you prepared a detailed report to show that the problematic legislation in the recipient jurisdiction identified by your assessment does not apply to the transferred data or the data importer in practice?","number":2,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's recent guidance if the data exporter reasonably believes that problematic legislations, in the recipient jurisdiction, identified through the assessment shall not be applied, in practice, to the transferred data and/or importer, thus not affecting the safeguards implemented within the Article 46 transfer tool, the data exporter may choose not to implement supplementary measures and may choose to proceed with the transfer. However, the data exporter, in collaboration with the data importer, must prepare a detailed report documenting the reasons why the problematic legislation will not be applied in practice to the transferred data and/or importer in question. Reports should be endorsed by the legal representative of the exporter and shall take into account:
*Comprehensive information on the legal assessment of the legislation and practices, and of their application to the specific transfers;
*The internal procedure to produce the assessment (including information on actors involved in the assessment-e.g. law firms, consultants, or internal departments, etc.) and dates of the checks;
*The experience of other actors operating within the same sector and/or related to similar transferred personal data;
*Other sources of information specified in Annex 3 of the EDPB's final recommendations on supplementary measures for cross-border transfer of data.

If the data exporter cannot implement supplementary measures to provide an 'essentially equivalent' level of protection to the transferred data, the data importer should be made to delete or return all the previously transferred data and all present and future data transfers to the recipient jurisdiction should be immediately suspended unless supplementary measures can be implemented (or are implemented). The EDPB requires that the deletion or return of previously transferred data and the suspension of all present and future transfers to the recipient jurisdiction should be recorded and documented for future data audits. It is also important to note that the data importer must delete the data in such a manner that there should be no backups or reasonably accessible technical measures for reconstitution of the deleted data (especially for the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction).","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk","Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["yes"],"risk":{"level":"Low","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf you reasonably conclude that problematic legislations of the recipient jurisdiction identified by you in your assessment will not, in practice, affect the transferred data or the data importer you may choose to carry out the transfer without undertaking supplementary measures. You must however document your reasoning and assessment very carefully and have it available in case of audits by your competent supervisory authorities. Please note that having the report still does not lower the risk of facing penalties if your reasoning is not found to be adequate and the transfer without supplemental measures is considered a violation of the EDPB standards which could result in you facing penalties and other regulatory and reputational risks.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you apply some supplementary measures to provide enhanced protection to the transferred data. This is so that you can mitigate the risk of your opinion turning out to be incorrect. It is important to note that if your analysis of the applicable legislation of the recipient jurisdiction indicates that access to data may take place even in the absence of the importer’s intervention, at the moment of transfer, necessary supplementary measures must be placed to protect personal data during its transmission and processing in the recipient jurisdiction (i.e end-to-end encryption).\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}},{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Very High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf you cannot undertake or have not undertaken supplementary measures to enhance protection of the transferred personal data to a non-EEA recipient jurisdiction with no adequacy decision, the legal analysis of which has revealed deficiencies and gaps in the implementation and effectiveness of safeguards contained in the Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to surveillance laws or practices which allow the recipient jurisdiction\u0026#39;s public authorities to surveil/access/acquire the transferred personal data in an unjustifiable manner - beyond the standards established by EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees) and you do not believe that practices of the public authorities in the recipient jurisdiction exempt the transferred data or the importer, then you must ensure that transferred personal data is deleted or returned and all future transfers are suspended, or you shall risk data subjects\u0026#39; data to be illegally accessed in violation of the GDPR. This shall make you liable to regulatory risks (penalties that can go up to 4% of your global revenues and suspensions of international transfers of data) as well data subjects\u0026#39; private rights of actions.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you do not believe that is the case or you cannot produce such a report, then you must suspend all current and future data transfers to the recipient jurisdiction and communicate to the data importer to return and delete the transferred data (including all backups) and to communicate this demand to any other entity within the same recipient jurisdiction it might have sent this data to as an onward transfer. If you cannot ensure the data importer has deleted or returned the data in a manner that the data subjects would no longer be at risk, it would be advised to inform and notify your relevant Supervisory Authority and the data subject. Record and document your request to the data importer to delete or return the transferred data and any other measures you have taken to ensure the data has been deleted or returned in a manner that no longer puts the transferred data at risk (i.e audit of the data importer\u0026#39;s facility and databases etc.).\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":2,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssrz0ye","title":"Have you employed or are you employing encryption to the transferred data in a manner that makes it an effective supplementary measure as per the EDPB?","number":3,"description":"","additional_details":"In its most recent guidance on international transfers, the EDPB has stressed on technical measures as the most important supplementary measures (supplemented by contractual or organizational measures) to protect the transferred data. For encryption, several conditional factors need to be considered, specifically the two points when the recipient jurisdiction's public authorities might try to surveil/access/acquire the personal information: during transit or when at rest. Thus, encryption must protect the data at both points. It is also important to note that the encryption must not be banned or made illegal by the law of the recipient jurisdiction (or mandate backdoors to be created for public authorities). The EDPB has not specified any checklist of factors that can ensure that encryption is an effective supplementary measure but in footnote 80 of its most recent guidance has stated 'data exporters can rely on technical guidance published by official cybersecurity authorities of the EU and its member states and from examples of use cases the following factors which should be considered can be inferred:

*Strong encryption before transmission;
*Encryption algorithm and its parameterization considered robust against third country’s cryptanalysis capabilities;
*Strength of the encryption takes into account the amount of time personal data is to be kept safe (The protective capacity of cryptographic algorithms is subject to decline over time due to the discovery of new cryptanalytic techniques - thus with time the protection offered by an obsolete encryption technique degrades);
*Encryption algorithm is flawlessly implemented (proper software, certification);
*Encryption keys reliably managed;
*Encryption keys are retained by data exporter or a third party based in EEA or an organization in a third country considered adequate;
*Encryption methodology is updated as per technological updates and constantly monitored;
*Transport encryption is used (when it is in transit);
*Decryption is only possible outside the recipient jurisdiction (when being routed from the recipient jurisdiction to another adequate jurisdiction);
*Use of a trustworthy public-key certification authority or infrastructure (when being routed from the recipient jurisdiction to another adequate jurisdiction);
*Specific protective and state-of-the-art measures against active and passive attacks employed (when being routed or in transit);
*Use end-to-end encryption if transport encryption is believed to be compromised (when being routed or in transit);
*No backdoors must exist in the software or hardware.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf the transferred personal data is not encrypted or is not encrypted in a manner that is effective in protecting it, it allows for it to be intercepted and accessible by third parties (most notably, public authorities in recipient jurisdictions). This poses risks for the data subjects and makes you liable for the violation of the GDPR, which can make you liable to regulatory risks (penalties that can go up to 4% of your global revenues and suspensions of international transfers of data) as well data subjects\u0026#39; private rights of actions.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are not employing encryption, in transit or at rest, for the transferred personal data, you should immediately undertake it. If your encryption methods and mechanisms are not up to par with the risk posed to the transferred data, the cryptography capabilities of the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction, the context of the data transfer, technological updates, and/or EDPB\u0026#39;s guidelines, they must be immediately updated. If encryption cannot be implemented up to those standards, other technical measures (supplemented by contractual and organizational measures) should be undertaken.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":3,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksss1dr8","title":"Have you pseudonymized or are you pseudonymizing the transferred data in a manner that makes it an effective supplementary measure as per the EDPB?","number":4,"description":"","additional_details":"In its most recent guidance on international transfers, the EDPB has stressed on technical measures as the most important supplementary measures (supplemented by contractual or organizational measures) to protect the transferred data. Pseudonymization is proposed as a possible technical supplementary measure data exporters can take when sending data to a third-party processor for trend or statistical analysis (any activity not requiring identifying information of the data subjects). Several conditional factors need to be considered, which are listed below (non-exhaustive):

*The data cannot be used anymore in any manner to re-identify the data subject without additional information;
*The additional information is retained by data exporter or a third party based in EEA or an organization in a third country considered adequate;
*Additional information and means of reidentifying the data adequately protected by safeguards (Additional data may consist of tables juxtaposing pseudonyms with the identifying attributes they replace, cryptographic keys or other parameters for the transformation of attributes, or other data permitting the attribution of the pseudonymized data to identified or identifiable natural persons);
*An analysis confirms that public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction cannot reidentify the data even if cross-referenced with other data they might possess.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf the transferred personal data is not pseudonymized (or the pseudonymization is not as sufficient or as robust as required by the EDPB) then the transferred data is at the risk of being intercepted by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction when it is being transferred to or when it arrives for a data importer or a third party processor for statistical or trend analysis and if the pseudonymization has been conducted but it is not robust, the intercepted data set can be subject to reidentification by the acquisition of the set of identifying information (if it is not properly stored or protected) or by cross-referencing the pseudonymized data with publically available data (or data available to public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). This would invite unneeded risk to data subjects\u0026#39; as their data and their identities would become available to a third party interceptor (including public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). This would make you liable for a violation of the GDPR and can open you up to regulatory risks (4% of global revenues and suspension of international data transfers) along with data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), for the purposes of statistical or trend analysis, then it is recommended you pseudonymize the data to remove identifying information, thus ensuring data subjects\u0026#39; privacy rights are not violated if the data is intercepted. If you are already employing pseudonymization, it is recommended you ensure it is robust and the pseudonymized data cannot be reasonably reidentified by any third party interceptor (including the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction) without having the set of identifying data (which should be protected, stored separately and unavailable to the third-party interceptors).\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":4,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksss4q5m","title":"Have you employed or are you planning to employ multi-party split processing in a manner that makes it an effective supplementary measure as per the EDPB?","number":5,"description":"","additional_details":"In its most recent guidance on international transfers, the EDPB has stressed technical measures as the most important supplementary measures (supplemented by contractual or organizational measures) to protect the transferred data. Multi-party split processing of transferred personal data is another mechanism you can utilize to continue to transfer data for processing by third-party service providers in recipient jurisdictions that are not in the EEA, do not have an adequacy rating, and whose legal systems cannot ensure an 'essential equivalent' level of protection to the transferred data without the employment of supplementary measures. The following conditions must be considered for this measure to be considered effective:

*The personal data must be split in a manner that it cannot be used for reidentification without the other set.
*The processors may jointly process the data sets but only if they use multi-party computation which has an algorithm secure against active attacks.
*There is no evidence of collaboration between the public authorities of the two countries, such that all sets of the data can be accessed by them and used to reconstitute the personal data.
*An analysis confirms that public authorities of third countries cannot reidentify the data in a set even if cross-referenced with other data they might possess.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf you can split data sets and send them to different processors to different jurisdictions for split processing, thus effectively ensuring that the transferred data even if intercepted by third party interceptors (including public authorities in the recipient jurisdiction) cannot be used to identify the data subjects to whom it relates to without the other set, you could effectively continue to transfer data to the recipient jurisdiction without risking data subjects\u0026#39; rights and freedoms, thus avoiding regulatory risks for yourself.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring data to a third party processor in recipient jurisdictions that are not in the EEA, do not have an adequacy rating, and whose legal systems cannot ensure an \u0026#39;essential equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection to the transferred data without the employment of supplementary measures consider splitting the data sets for multiparty split processing, especially where encryption or some other technical measure for the protection of the data is not possible or is not enough.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":5,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksss7j0t","title":"Have you employed or are you planning to employ any other technical measures in a manner that makes them an effective supplementary measure to protect the data?","number":6,"description":"","additional_details":"In its most recent guidance on international transfers, the EDPB has stressed on technical measures as the most important supplementary measures (supplemented by contractual or organizational measures) to protect the transferred data. Technical measures must address the risk to the transferred data created by legal gaps and deficiencies discovered in the legal assessment of the recipient jurisdiction and should help achieve an 'essentially equivalent' protection for the transferred data.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eTechnical measures can help protect the transferred data from being intercepted or surveilled or can ensure it is useless to the interceptor if accessed (even by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). Thus by not undertaking them, you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as you could against third-party interceptors (including public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction) who are not bound to honor the contractual or organizational measures you undertake with the data importer.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), consider taking additional technical measures to provide additional supplementary protection to the transferred data.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":6,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksssgzol","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual obligations upon the data importer to implement the technical measures you have proposed above?","number":7,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Bringing agreed-upon technical measures into the legal form will be helpful in enforcing them due to the risk of the penalty attached with non-compliance by the data importer.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures supplement technical measures you have adopted to protect the transferred data by binding data importers to implement these measures or face legal action for breach of contract. By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as you could as the data importer would face no risk of penalty upon breaking its obligations to undertake steps to implement these measures. This poses risks for the data subjects and makes you liable for the violation of the GDPR, which can make you liable to regulatory risks (penalties that can go up to 4% of your global revenues and suspensions of international transfers of data) as well data subjects\u0026#39; private rights of actions\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you undertake technical measures which are further supplemented by contractual measures - beyond the protections provided by the Article 46 tools (which are also contractual in nature) - which require the data importer to implement the agreed-upon technical measures or face penalties for breach of contract.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":7,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksssjpxi","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual transparency obligations upon the data importer to inform data exporters of the surveillance laws of the recipient jurisdiction?","number":8,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Thus data importers can be obligated to:
*Disclose and enumerate to the data exporter the laws, regulations, and practices in the recipient jurisdiction that allow public authorities to access the transferred data (i.e for law enforcement, intelligence, administrative, and regulatory supervision purposes). When the law is not clear - common practices, judicial decisions, practical experience, third party accounts can be relied upon;

*Indicate measures taken to prevent access to the data;
*Provide detailed information of past surveillance/access/acquisition requests made by public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction through structured questionnaires or scheduled declarations to the data exporter:
**Purpose for which access sought
**Information of request received
**Data requested to be accessed
**Requesting body
**Legal basis for the disclosure
**What extent disclosure has been made
**Specify to what extent it is legally prohibited to provide the above-mentioned information to the data exporter as per the law of recipient jurisdiction.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. Transparency obligations ensure that data importers provide transparent information to data exporters on whether any surveillance/access requests for the transferred data by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction have been received in the past or not. By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you undertake contractual measures imposing strict transparency obligations to ensure that you have completed your due diligence before transferring data to the data importer.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":8,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksssnkki","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual transparency obligations upon the data importer to certify to the data exporter that no back doors exist in their systems?","number":9,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Thus data importers can be obligated to certify that:

*There are no back doors in the data importers' systems that allow access to any third party interceptor (including the public authorities of the recipient country);
*There is no law in the recipient jurisdiction which prohibits the data importer from informing the data exporter of any backdoors or access channels for public authorities;
*There must be penalties or right of data exporter to cancel the contract if such backdoors are discovered later on the data importers' systems.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. Transparency obligations ensure that data importers do not have any backdoors in their systems which can allow third-party interceptors such as the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction from surveilling/accessing or acquiring the data. By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you undertake contractual measures imposing strict transparency obligations to ensure there are no backdoors in the data importer\u0026#39;s systems which can allow third-party interceptors (such as the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction) from surveilling, accessing or acquiring the transferred data.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":9,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksssprtz","title":"Have you contractually obligated transparency obligations on the data importer to allow enhanced audits?","number":10,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Thus data importers can be obligated to allow enhanced audits with the following conditions:

*The purpose of the audits is to verify if the data has been disclosed to public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction;
*The audits can be on-site or remote audits/inspections of data importer’s facilities;
*There shall be short notice and rapid intervention by the auditors/inspectors; 
*Selection of auditors/inspectors will solely lie with you (the data exporter);
*Scope of the audit should cover any processing by the data importer’s processors or sub-processors as well (in the same recipient jurisdiction or in another country).","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. Transparency obligations requiring enhanced audits ensure that data importers are complying with obligations imposed upon them to protect the transferred data while also ensuring the transferred data has not been subject to surveillance or access by any third party interceptor (especially the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you undertake contractual measures imposing enhanced audits to ensure the data importer has taken all the steps it has promised to take to protect the transferred data in its facilities.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":10,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssssevt","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual transparency obligations upon the data importer to notify the data exporter if it can no longer meet its contractual commitments for the protection of the data?","number":11,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Changes in legislation and practices in a recipient jurisdiction can take place immediately and they can affect the effectiveness of the Article 46 transfer protections for the transferred data, the supplementary measures taken to ensure the protection of the data, and ultimately, the security and confidentiality of the data itself. Thus, data exporters can contractually obligate data importers to inform the data exporter if such changes affect their capability to meet their contractual commitments towards protecting the transferred data. Other factors to consider are:

*The data importer should be required to carefully monitor any legal or policy changes in the recipient jurisdiction which will affect the protection of the transferred data and take swift action to notify the data exporter before they are implemented;
*The notification of a change in surveillance law or practice needs to take place before access is granted to public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction;
*The data importer can also be obligated to promptly encrypt the remaining transferred data after the notification is made and send it back to the data exporter or delete the data within a fixed time frame;
*These mechanisms should be regularly audited, tested, and verified.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. Transparency obligations requiring notification in case of inability to comply with contractual commitment ensure that data importers are complying with obligations imposed upon them to protect the transferred data while also ensuring the transferred data has not been subject to surveillance or access by any third party interceptor (especially the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you undertake contractual measures imposing a duty on data importers to timely and transparently notify data exporters if the law or practice of the recipient jurisdiction is being changed or has been changed in a manner which makes it impossible for the data importer to implement the protection measures for the transferred data.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":11,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksssvt21","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual transparency obligations upon the data importer to employ 'warrant canaries'/passive notification systems for government surveillance requests?","number":12,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Warrant canaries are a form of passive notification systems used by entities to declare transparently on whether they have ever received a government surveillance request or not. While surveillance laws generally prohibit an entity to reveal the fact that it has received a surveillance request, especially in any detailed manner which can threaten the act of surveillance/access/acquisition of the data by the public authorities, a passive system which lets the public (or data exporters) know that the data importer has never received a data surveillance request on the data but then stops relaying that information when a surveillance request is received, is arguably not illegal or prohibited. The following conditions must be considered though:

*The passive notification must be updated every 24 hours (or earlier) and cryptographically signed by the data importer;
*The recipient jurisdiction’s laws and regulations must allow for such ‘warrant canary’/passive notification mechanisms and should not be able to force the data importer to issue fake ‘warrant canary’/passive notification updates;
*Data exporter must monitor such communications regularly;
*Data importer must make sure its private key and cryptographic signatures are secure and it is signed by several persons (some in another jurisdiction other than the recipient jurisdiction).","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. Transparency obligations requiring warrant canaries ensure that data importers are complying with obligations imposed upon them to protect the transferred data while also ensuring the transferred data has not been subject to surveillance or access by any third party interceptor (especially the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you require the data importer to implement a passive notification system to monitor if any surveillance or access request by the public authorities has been made or action has been taken in relation to the transferred data and the law of the recipient jurisdiction does not allow the direct notification.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":12,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksssy1ay","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual obligations upon the data importer to mandatorily review or challenge illegal or unjustified surveillance requests made by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction?","number":13,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Data importers can be contractually obligated to review and legally challenge surveillance/access requests or actions taken by the public authorities of the recipient country in whichever appropriate forum is available if they reach the conclusion that the request or actions by the public authorities are illegal or overbroad. This can ensure that unjustified intrusions and interceptions of the transferred data shall face increased scrutiny and it can also act as a mechanism to make the existence of the surveillance/access requests for the transferred data by the public authorities public knowledge. It also shall allow data exporters and importers to show a serious commitment towards protecting the transferred data as any legal challenge to a surveillance/access request shall involve spending a significant amount of resources. It should be noted that all the details of the review process and the consequent challenge should be documented and recorded for compliance purposes. Other factors to account for, to ensure this is an effective supplementary measure are:

*The legal order of the recipient jurisdiction must allow data importers to pose such challenges before legal (or similar) forums against surveillance requests/actions by public authorities;
*This contractual measure will necessarily need to be complementary to other supplementary measures;
*Challenges to the order must have an immediate suspensive effect (interim suspension of the surveillance request or action).
*The most minimum amount of data required by the public authorities should be handed over even if the challenge fails (or it does not have an immediate suspensive effect).","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. Contractual obligations requiring data importers to mandatorily review or challenge illegal or unjustified surveillance requests made by public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction ensure that data importers are complying with obligations imposed upon them to protect the transferred data while also ensuring the transferred data has not been subject to surveillance or access by any third party interceptor (especially the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you obligate data importers to review surveillance and access requests sent and the actions taken by the public authorities of the recipient country. The data importers shall also be obligated to challenge surveillance requests and actions of the public authorities of the recipient country where the data is stored, which are found to be unjustified or overbroad.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":13,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksst0dcw","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual obligations upon the data importer to mandatorily review and inform the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction of the incompatibility of the surveillance request with the safeguards in the Article 46 tool used to transfer the data?","number":14,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. In case an unjustified or disproportionate surveillance request is received by the data importer for the access/acquisition of the transferred data by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction, the data importer can be contractually obligated to inform the public authorities of the application of the GDPR Article 46 on the data and thus the need to protect the data from surveillance or access unless the EU essential guarantees are not met by the recipient jurisdiction's surveillance laws. The information provided to public authorities or courts of the recipient jurisdiction of the conflicting legal requirements on the transferred data can have some legal effect and invite pause and restraint on their part. It could also lead to a higher level of scrutiny: judicial review of the surveillance request, the requirement for a warrant for the acquisition of the data, suspension of the surveillance request, etc. The following factors need to be kept in mind:

*Data importers must also inform the data exporters or the appropriate supervisory authority in the EEA of the surveillance/access request;
*The legal order of the recipient jurisdiction must allow the data importer to inform the data exporter or relevant Supervisory Authority of the data surveillance/access request;
*Documentation of best efforts from the data importer would be required.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. A contractual obligation for the data importer to inform the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction which have made a surveillance or access request for the transferred data that the data contains EU residents data and the surveillance request is incompatible with the protections afforded to the data by the Article 46 transfer tool can ensure that there is higher-level scrutiny which is applied by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction before enforcing the surveillance or access request. By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you obligate data importers to inform public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction of the fact that the data contains EU residents data and the surveillance/access request is incompatible with the protections provided by the Article 46 transfer tool.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":14,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksst3dk9","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual obligations upon the data importer to seek the consent of the concerned data subject or the data exporter before voluntarily releasing the transferred data due to a surveillance request by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction?","number":15,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. In instances where a data importer receives a surveillance request from the public authorities of a recipient jurisdiction the compliance of which is voluntary and not compulsory, the data importer can be obligated to seek the data exporter's or the concerned data subjects' consent before releasing the information requested. It should be noted that:

*In certain situations the data subject cannot freely provide consent as per EU law and thus this measure shall not suffice;
*This might be only applicable if the recipient jurisdiction allows a data importer to inform a data exporter or data subject about data surveillance/access requests by public authorities and there are some technical measures available for the data exporter or data subject to shut down the data importer’s access to data stored in the EEA.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. A contractual obligation for the data importer to seek the consent of the data subject before voluntarily releasing data to public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction in case of receiving a surveillance/access request can help ensure data subject participation and choice. By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you obligate data importers to seek data subjects\u0026#39; consent before voluntarily complying with surveillance or access requests by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":15,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksst60tj","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual obligations upon the data importer to inform the concerned data subject or the data exporter of a surveillance/access request by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction for the transferred data so that the data subjects may seek redressal?","number":16,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. For this supplementary measure to be effective: 

*The notification to the concerned data subject (through the data importer or the data exporter) should be prompt and data subjects should be able to seek more information from the data exporter and inform their concerned Supervisory Authority;
*Information should be provided to the data subject to seek other redressals in the recipient jurisdiction (legal remedies etc.);
*This clause may also address the standing of data subject to challenge the surveillance requests by public authorities before the courts of the recipient jurisdiction;
*The notification to the data subject or data exporter by the data importer upon receiving a surveillance/access request from the public authorities should be legally permitted;
*If there is a prohibition on the data importer to notify, it should do so as soon as the prohibition ends or it should try its best to gain a waiver.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. A contractual obligation for the data importer to promptly inform the concerned data subject in case of receiving a surveillance/access request from the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction can help ensure data subject awareness and participation. By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you obligate data importers to inform the concerned data subjects when a surveillance or access request for their data is made by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":16,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksst8606","title":"Have you imposed or are you planning to impose contractual obligations upon yourself or the data importer to provide legal assistance or ad-hoc redressal mechanisms to the data subjects if a surveillance request is received by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction?","number":17,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, the EDPB has suggested that contractual measures should be put in place to supplement the technical measures and legally obligate the data importer to take steps to protect the data. Providing assistance to the data subject to seek redressal either through an ad-hoc redressal mechanism or the recipient jurisdiction's legal system (i.e relevant judicial forums) can be a very effective measure if the following factors are accounted for:

*Ad-hoc redress mechanisms should be allowed under the recipient jurisdiction's laws;
*Providing legal assistance and counseling  to the data subject could be very helpful but it is not a remedy in itself - it must complement other safeguards;
*A redressal mechanism for affected individuals must exist in the recipient jurisdiction against public authority access of personal data for the data subject to participate in for this measure to be effective.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eContractual measures bind data importers to implement specific agreed-upon measures and protections while ensuring they abide by their obligations or face legal action for breach of contract. A contractual obligation for the data importer to provide legal assistance or ad-hoc redressal mechanisms to challenge surveillance/access requests by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction is a strong measure to ensure data subjects\u0026#39; rights are respected and unjustified surveillance requests on the transferred data are appropriately challenged. By not undertaking any contractual measures you are not protecting the transferred data as adequately as the data importer will not feel obligated to honor this obligation honestly and fairly due to a lack of penalty.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you obligate data importers to provide affected data subjects legal assistance or ad-hoc redressal mechanisms to challenge the unjustified surveillance and access requests sent by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":17,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksstrmhk","title":"Have you or the data importer implemented or are planning to implement organizational measures such as the creation of internal policies for data transfers and SOPs along with reporting channels for handling data access requests by public authorities of recipient jurisdiction (specialized team creation, adequate training, etc.)?","number":18,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, organizational measures which can be applied by the data exporters and imposed on data importers contribute to ensuring consistency in the protection of personal data during the full cycle of the processing, while improving risk awareness and the capacity to react to surveillance requests by public authorities. By developing standard procedures and training staff on how to handle surveillance/access requests from public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction, the data exporter and importer shall be better placed to provide protection to the transferred data. The training should include the laws and practices of the recipient jurisdiction as well the EU requirements for such surveillance to be justified (i.e standards following from Article 52 (1) of the Charter of Fundamental Rights). The training also has to be regularly updated with any new developments in EU law as well as law and practices of the recipient jurisdiction and it should focus on assessment of practical examples of situations involving surveillance requests by public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction requiring application of the EU standards. The training should be specific to the type of data transferred to the data importer and the industry in which the data importer is based. It should be noted that this measure shall only apply as an effective measure where the surveillance laws and practices of the recipient jurisdiction are compatible with EU law.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eOrganizational measures supplement technical and contractual measures as they provide standards and practices both data exporters and importers can implement to ensure that the transferred data is protected during the full lifecycle of processing. By not requiring the data importer to adopt internal policies on data transfers and creating standards on how to react to data surveillance requests from the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction or by not adopting such policies and standards yourself, you are not protecting the transferred data in a holistic manner.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you implement and/or make the data importer implement internal policies, SOPs and reporting mechanisms for data transfer requests from public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":0,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":18,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1ksstxtvw","title":"Have you or the data importer implemented or are planning to implement organizational measures such as documenting and recording surveillance requests received from the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction?","number":19,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, organizational measures which can be applied by the data exporters and imposed on data importers contribute to ensuring consistency in the protection of personal data during the full cycle of the processing, while improving risk awareness and the capacity to react to surveillance requests by public authorities. Recording and documenting data surveillance/access requests from the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction is a good practice for accountability. These records should be provided to the data exporter who can then forward them to the concerned data subjects upon request. The following points need to be recorded:

*The surveillance/access request received from the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction;
*The response provided to the public authorities in reply to the request;
*The legal reasoning applied to accept the request and provide the public authorities access to the transferred data;
*The actors involved - if the data exporter had been notified of the request (unless the law of the recipient jurisdiction prohibits it) then an assessment of its team should also be attached.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eOrganizational measures supplement technical and contractual measures as they provide standards and practices both data exporters and importers can implement to ensure that the transferred data is protected during the full lifecycle of processing. By not obligating the data importer to document or record surveillance requests received by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction and not recording them yourself, you are at risk of data subjects\u0026#39; private right of action or regulatory action as you have no record of the interactions and actions taken by the data importer in response to the request.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you and the data importer begin to keep a record of all surveillance requests received by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction for accountability purposes.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":19,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssu15zl","title":"Have you or the data importer implemented or are planning to implement organizational measures such as regular publication of transparency reports or summaries of surveillance/access requests received by public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction?","number":20,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, organizational measures which can be applied by the data exporters and imposed on data importers contribute to ensuring consistency in the protection of personal data during the full cycle of the processing, while improving risk awareness and the capacity to react to surveillance requests by public authorities. The regular publication of transparency reports on surveillance/access requests received by public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction and the replies submitted by you or the data importer to such requests (as far as the law of the recipient jurisdiction allows) can be excellent step towards transparency of processing activities. The following factors need to be accounted for:

*The reports should be relevant, detailed, and clear;
*They can be statistical and aggregate data if not specific;
*The law of the recipient jurisdiction must not prohibit the data importer to publish these reports.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eOrganizational measures supplement technical and contractual measures as they provide standards and practices both data exporters and importers can implement to ensure that the transferred data is protected during the full lifecycle of processing. By not obligating the data importer to publish transparency reports, you are not aware of the extent of the surveillance and access requests received by the data importer from the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you require the data importer begin to publish transparency reports.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":0,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":20,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssu3zun","title":"Have you or the data importer implemented or are planning to implement organizational measures such as data minimization policies along with other organizational requirements under the GDPR accountability principles?","number":21,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, organizational measures which can be applied by the data exporters and imposed on data importers contribute to ensuring consistency in the protection of personal data during the full cycle of the processing, while improving risk awareness and the capacity to react to surveillance requests by public authorities. Data minimization and other accountability principles of the GDPR should be operationalized within your organization (and the data importers) as they help mitigate the harm to the data subject if the transferred data is compromised. Factors to ensure increased effectiveness are:

*There should be regular audits to ensure compliance with the principles and strong disciplinary actions for non-compliance;
*Data not needed to be transferred should not be transferred by the data exporter;
*This measure must supplement other measures to provide holistic protection to the transferred data.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eOrganizational measures supplement technical and contractual measures as they provide standards and practices both data exporters and importers can implement to ensure that the transferred data is protected during the full lifecycle of processing. By not obligating the data importer to implement data minimization policies and other GDPR accountability practices or not adopting them yourself, you are at risk of greater liability in case of future breaches or access of the transferred data by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you and the data importer implement data minimization measures along with other data processing principles from the GDPR.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":21,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssvcmy8","title":"Have you implemented or are planning to implement organizational measures such as the development of and adherence to the best practice of consulting with your DPO before a transfer?","number":22,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, organizational measures which can be applied by the data exporters and imposed on data importers contribute to ensuring consistency in the protection of personal data during the full cycle of the processing, while improving risk awareness and the capacity to react to surveillance requests by public authorities. Data exporters must institute a policy of review from their DPO before transferring data to a recipient jurisdiction. The following conditions must be met for this policy to be effective:

*The DPO has to be consulted before the transfer is made to the recipient jurisdiction;
*The DPO is to be provided access to the personal data and must assess if the transfer is necessary;
*The DPO must also assess the law of the recipient jurisdiction and suggest supplementary measures to protect the data.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Low","description":"\u003cp\u003eOrganizational measures supplement technical and contractual measures as they provide standards and practices both data exporters and importers can implement to ensure that the transferred data is protected during the full lifecycle of processing. By not gaining the advice of the DPO before transferring data, you are removing an important layer of oversight.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you gain the advice and approval of the DPO before transferring data.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":22,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssu85er","title":"Have you or the data importer implemented or are planning to implement organizational measures such as the adoption of strict data protection and privacy policies?","number":23,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, organizational measures which can be applied by the data exporters and imposed on data importers contribute to ensuring consistency in the protection of personal data during the full cycle of the processing, while improving risk awareness and the capacity to react to surveillance requests by public authorities. The data protection and privacy policies:

*Must be based on EU certification or codes of conduct or on international standards (e.g. ISO norms) and best practices (e.g. ENISA);
*Should have due regard to the state of the art technologies, in accordance with the risk of the categories of data processed and the likelihood of attempts from public authorities to access it.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Low","description":"\u003cp\u003eOrganizational measures supplement technical and contractual measures as they provide standards and practices both data exporters and importers can implement to ensure that the transferred data is protected during the full lifecycle of processing. By not adopting state-of-the-art, EU-certified, or internationally recognized data privacy and security policies nor having the data importer adopt them, you are not taking all the steps you can to protect the transferred data.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you consider adopting state-of-the-art, EU certified or internationally recognized data privacy and security policies or obligate your data importers to do so as well.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":23,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssub7db","title":"Have you or the data importer implemented or are planning to implement organizational measures such as the regular review and update of internal policies and practices so that they remain suitable safeguards to protect against the threats posed to the data?","number":24,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, organizational measures which can be applied by the data exporters and imposed on data importers contribute to ensuring consistency in the protection of personal data during the full cycle of the processing, while improving risk awareness and the capacity to react to surveillance requests by public authorities. Constant review and updating of policies will ensure that your measures are never obsolete and remain always effective.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eOrganizational measures supplement technical and contractual measures as they provide standards and practices both data exporters and importers can implement to ensure that the transferred data is protected during the full lifecycle of processing. By not obligating the data importer to adopt a policy to review and update internal policies or adopting such policies yourself, you are not protecting the transferred data in a holistic manner as the standards and practices adopted by you and data importer could become obsolete or useless due to any change in the law or practice of the recipient jurisdiction or in the EU/EDPB guidelines on cross border data transfers.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you consider adopting policies for constant review and update of internal procedures and standards to deal with data surveillance/access requests by the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction or obligate your data importers to implement such review policies.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":24,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssue2x6","title":"Have you obligated the data importer to commit to not transfer the transferred data further onwards?","number":25,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's latest guidance on international data transfers, data exporters are to ensure supplementary measures are applied before the transfer of personal data is made to a non-adequate jurisdiction outside of the EEA which has a legal system that will not enforce the Article 46 protections nor provide an 'essentially equivalent' level of protection to the transferred data. But these supplementary measures can be undone if the data importer further transmits or shares the data onwards to other data importers (i.e subprocessors etc.) within the same recipient jurisdiction or another similar recipient jurisdiction. Thus, it is best to require a commitment from the data importer, that the transferred data shall not be subject to onwards transfers to other data importers.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf the data importer can further transfer the data to another data importer in the same recipient jurisdiction or to a data importer in another non-adequate jurisdiction outside the EEA, it shall make the legal analysis conducted by you and the supplementary measures adopted to protect the transferred data useless as they will not apply to the new data importer and will not be adopted to cover the gaps in the legal order of the new recipient jurisdiction. This can open you up to liabilities such as the fines for violating the GDPR (going up to 4% of global revenues) and data subjects\u0026#39; private actions.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIf you are transferring personal data to a recipient jurisdiction outside of the EEA area, which does not have an adequacy decision and has a legal system that cannot guarantee the effectiveness of protections provided to the data under the employed Article 46 transfer tool (especially due to public surveillance laws which do not match the standards of EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees), it is recommended that you ensure that data shall not be forwarded by the data importer to another entity in the same jurisdiction or to another non-adequate jurisdiction outside the EEA.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":25,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssui8kn","title":"Are the supplementary measures you have taken to protect the data effective and do they cover the legal gaps/deficiencies uncovered in the legal assessment of the recipient jurisdiction?","number":26,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB's recent guidance, if the supplementary measures undertaken by the data exporter do not provide an 'essentially equivalent' level of protection to the transferred data, the data importer should be made to delete or return all the previously transferred data and all present and future data transfers to the recipient jurisdiction should be immediately suspended unless supplementary measures can be implemented (or are implemented). The EDPB requires that the deletion or return of previously transferred data and the suspension of all present and future transfers to the recipient jurisdiction should be recorded and documented for future data audits. It is also important to note that the data importer must delete the data in such a manner that there should be no backups or reasonably accessible technical measures for reconstitution of the deleted data (especially for the public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction). While the EDPB does not provide a tiered approach, we have provided responses and based resultant risk and recommendations on a proportionality basis as per the spirit of the GDPR.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Multi-Select","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes, they are effective and provide adequate cover","value":"Choice 1","shortcut":""},{"label":"Most major gaps have been covered","value":"Choice 2","shortcut":""},{"label":"Cover has been provided to a certain extent but some gaps remain","value":"Choice 3","shortcut":""},{"label":"Most major gaps remain","value":"Choice 4","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-multi-selectbox","tableView":true,"type":"selectboxes","key":"multiSelect","input":true,"defaultValue":{"Choice 1":false,"Choice 2":false,"Choice 3":false,"Choice 4":false},"multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["multiSelect"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["Choice 4"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eIf the recipient jurisdiction does not have an adequacy decision and transfers are based on Article 46 transfer tools and the legal analysis of the recipient jurisdiction has revealed deficiencies that would make the Article 46 safeguards unimplementable or unenforceable especially against public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction which can surveil/access/acquire the transferred personal data in excess of the standards established by the EDPB\u0026#39;s guidance on EU essential guarantees, the deficiencies and gaps must be covered by implementing supplementary measures (technical measures supplemented by contractual and organizational measures). \u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThese measures must directly address the gaps and deficiencies uncovered. If they do not, the transferred data is at risk and you would be in violation of the GDPR to allow the data transfer to continue.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is important that you suspend all current and future data transfers to the recipient jurisdiction and communicate to the data importer to return and delete the transferred data (including all back ups) and to communicate this demand to any other entity within the same recipient jurisdiction it might have sent this data to as an onward transfer. \u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIf you cannot ensure the data importer has deleted or returned the data in a manner that the data subjects would no longer be at risk, it would be advised to inform and notify your relevant Supervisory Authority and the data subject. Record and document your request to the data importer to delete or return the transferred data and any other measures you have taken to ensure the data has been deleted or returned in a manner that no longer puts the transferred data at risk (i.e audit of the data importer\u0026#39;s facility and databases etc.).\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":26,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null}],"data_map_metadata":null,"edit_permissions":{"OWNER":false,"USER":false,"COLLABORATOR":false}},{"id":0,"heading":"Procedural Steps","number":6,"category":"","questions":[{"qid":"question-1kssul0rd","title":"Which transfer tool are you utilizing?","number":1,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB requires all data exporters to undertake a transfer impact assessment for any and all transfer tools relied upon under Article 46 of the GDPR to conduct transfers to non-adequate jurisdictions outside of the EEA, however, each transfer tool might provide its own protections which must be held in mind when undertaking a legal assessment of the recipient jurisdiction to see if the data has been provided 'essentially equivalent' protection.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Multi-Select","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)","value":"Choice 1","shortcut":""},{"label":"Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs)","value":"Choice 2","shortcut":""},{"label":"Standard data protection clauses adopted by a supervisory authority and approved by the Commission","value":"Choice 3","shortcut":""},{"label":"Certification under an approved certification mechanism together with binding and enforceable commitments of the receiver outside the EEA","value":"Choice 4","shortcut":""},{"label":"Contractual clauses authorised by a supervisory authority","value":"contractualClausesAuthorisedByASupervisoryAuthority","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-multi-selectbox","tableView":true,"type":"selectboxes","key":"multiSelect","input":true,"defaultValue":{"Choice 1":false,"Choice 2":false,"Choice 3":false,"Choice 4":false},"multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":null,"responseTypes":["multiSelect"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":null,"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":1,"skip_parent":"","skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssuqx33","title":"Have you or you are planning to take the necessary procedural steps to implement the supplementary measures you have decided to utilize within the SCCs?","number":2,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB in its most recent guidance on international data transfers requires different procedural steps to be taken by data exporters to implement the supplementary measures as the Schrems II decision affects all transfer instruments under Article 46 of the GDPR due to their overt contractual nature. As per the EDPB in the case of SCCs, there is no need to seek the approval of the relevant Supervisory Authority if the supplementary measures being incorporated do not restrict the rights of the data subjects or contradict the obligations of the data exporter and importer under the SCCs or lower the level of data protection for the transferred data. It is the responsibility of the data exporter under the accountability principle of the GDPR to show these additions are unambiguously strengthening the rights of data subjects under the SCCs and not contradicting the SCCs in any manner. In case of receiving a request of review from the data exporter or a complaint from the data subject, the relevant Supervisory Authority could review these additions.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eThe EDPB requires all data exporters to immediately implement supplementary measures within the SCCs since additions into the SCCs do not contradict the rights of the data subject or existing obligations of the data exporter or importer need not be reviewed or approved by the Supervisory Authority. Not implementing these measures within the SCCs or implementing them in a manner where the SCCs are weakened as a whole could be construed as a violation of the GDPR and open you up to regulatory risk (with fines going up to 4% of global revenues and blockage of all future international data transfers) and data subjects\u0026#39; private actions.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended you conduct a thorough review of your SCCs with data importers in non-adequate jurisdictions outside the EEA which do not provide \u0026#39;an essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection to the transferred data and incorporate most, if not all, of your decided supplementary measures within the SCCs, after ensuring they are not contradictory to the protections offered to the transferred data by the SCCs as a whole. Document and record your reasons for adding the clauses and how you believe they strengthen the existing protections granted by the SCCs. If you deem it necessary, you may request your Supervisory Authority to also review these additions.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":2,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssutmvm","title":"Have you or you planning to take the necessary procedural steps to implement the supplementary measures you have decided to utilize within the BCRs?","number":3,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB in its most recent guidance on international data transfers requires different procedural steps to be taken by data exporters to implement the supplementary measures as the Schrems II decision affects all transfer instruments under Article 46 of the GDPR due to their overt contractual nature. For BCRs, the EDPB recommends data exporters undertake all these steps and implement the appropriate supplementary measures. The EDPB shall release more detailed guidance for the need to include additional commitments in the BCRs soon.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eThe EDPB has suggested (but not directed) all data exporters using BCRs might have to incorporate supplementary measures within the BCRs and failure to do so could be construed as a violation of the GDPR and open data exporters up to regulatory risk (with fines going up to 4% of global revenues and blockage of all future international data transfers) and data subjects\u0026#39; private actions.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended you consider incorporating the supplementary measures within BCRs to continue data transfers to data importers in non-adequate jurisdictions outside the EEA which do not provide \u0026#39;an essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection to the transferred data. Or at the very least keep up to date with the latest guidance released by the EDPB on international transfers and the effect of Schrems II on the BCRs.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssuwcl0","title":"Have you or you planning to take the necessary procedural steps to implement the supplementary measures you have decided to utilize within the ad-hoc contractual clauses?","number":4,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB in its most recent guidance on international data transfers requires different procedural steps to be taken by data exporters to implement the supplementary measures as the Schrems II decision affects all transfer instruments under Article 46 of the GDPR due to their overt contractual nature. For ad-hoc contractual clauses, the EDPB recommends data exporters undertake all these steps and implement the appropriate supplementary measures. The EDPB shall release more detailed guidance for ad-hoc contractual clauses soon.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eThe EDPB has suggested (but not directed) all data exporters using ad-hoc contractual clauses might have to incorporate supplementary measures within those clauses and failure to do so could be construed as a violation of the GDPR and open up data exporters to regulatory risk (with fines going up to 4% of global revenues and blockage of all future international data transfers) and data subjects\u0026#39; private actions.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended you consider incorporating the supplementary measures within the ad-hoc contractual clauses to continue data transfers to data importers in non-adequate jurisdictions outside the EEA which do not provide \u0026#39;an essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection to the transferred data. Or at the very least keep up to date with the latest guidance released by the EDPB on international transfers and the effect of Schrems II on the ad-hoc contractual clauses used for data transfers.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":null,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null},{"qid":"question-1kssuy8by","title":"Have you or you planning to take the necessary procedural steps to implement the supplementary measures you have decided to utilize within the Article 46 transfer tool?","number":5,"description":"","additional_details":"The EDPB in its most recent guidance on international data transfers requires data exporters to take different procedural steps for the implementation of supplementary measures. Please consult your respective supervisory authority to know what particular procedural steps you are required to take.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"Medium","description":"\u003cp\u003eThe EDPB has suggested (but not directed) all data exporters using Article 46 transfer tools might have to incorporate supplementary measures within those clauses and failure to do so could be construed as a violation of the GDPR and open up data exporters to regulatory risk (with fines going up to 4% of global revenues and blockage of all future international data transfers) and data subjects\u0026#39; private actions.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended you consider incorporating the supplementary measures within the Article 46 transfer tool to continue data transfers to data importers in non-adequate jurisdictions outside the EEA which do not provide \u0026#39;an essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection to the transferred data. Or at the very least keep up to date with the latest guidance released by the EDPB on international transfers and the effect of Schrems II on the ad-hoc contractual clauses used for data transfers.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"workflow_mapping":null,"skip_mapping":null,"data_map_metadata":null}","tags":["Data Transfer Impact Assessments"],"displayNumber":5,"skip_parent":null,"skip_number":null}],"data_map_metadata":null,"edit_permissions":{"OWNER":false,"USER":false,"COLLABORATOR":false}},{"id":0,"heading":"Monitor and Review","number":7,"category":"","questions":[{"qid":"question-1kssv3vah","title":"Are you regularly monitoring and reviewing supplementary measures that you have undertaken with an aim to address the gaps of the legal system of the recipient's jurisdiction?","number":1,"description":"","additional_details":"As per the EDPB, data exporters must implement measures that ensure that the transferred data is always provided an 'essentially equivalent' level of protection in the recipient jurisdiction. This involves constant evaluation and monitoring of the sufficiency of the supplementary measures to overcome or cover the gaps found in the legal system of the recipient jurisdiction, which do not allow the safeguards enshrined under the Article 46 tools to be effective (especially against public authorities of the recipient jurisdiction which wish to surveil/access/acquire the transferred data). As per the EDPB, this responsibility falls under the accountability principle of the GDPR, and data exporters need to be constantly vigilant for any change in circumstances, practices or laws, or regulations in the recipient jurisdiction which could threaten the transferred data and suspend all data transfers and have the transferred data deleted if: 

*The importer has breached or is unable to honor its commitments under the Article 46 transfer tool;
*The supplementary measures enacted by the data exporter are no longer effective in the recipient jurisdiction.","mandatory":false,"custom_builder_response":"{"builder":[{"label":"Yes/No","hideLabel":true,"optionsLabelPosition":"right","values":[{"label":"Yes","value":"yes","shortcut":""},{"label":"No","value":"no","shortcut":""}],"customClass":"f-boolean-selector","tableView":true,"type":"radio","key":"yesNo","input":true,"defaultValue":"","multiple":false,"refreshOn":"","clearOnRefresh":false,"placeholder":""}],"rules_order":["Set Risk"],"responseTypes":["yesNo"],"rating_mapping":null,"risk_mapping":{"rules":[{"operator":"Equals to","value":["no"],"risk":{"level":"High","description":"\u003cp\u003eEDPB requires constant vigilance from the data exporter and data importer to ensure that the safeguards provided to the transferred data through the Article 46 transfer tool and the supplementary measures remain effective in the recipient jurisdiction to provide an \u0026#39;essentially equivalent\u0026#39; level of protection. If you do not monitor and review the recipient jurisdiction\u0026#39;s legal system, EU and EDPB guidelines, and the effectiveness of the protections, you could be found liable for the violation of the GDPR and could face regulatory risks (fines up to 4% of global revenues and an injunction against all future data transfers) as well as private actions by data subjects.\u003c/p\u003e","recommendation":"\u003cp\u003eIt is recommended that you implement a system of constant review and monitoring of the recipient jurisdiction\u0026#39;s legal system and EU and EDPB guidelines to assess the effectiveness of the protections through the Article 46 transfer tool. The data importer can be required to implement this system and provide assistance. A third-party service (such as a law firm) can also be contracted to provide this service.\u003c/p\u003e","owner":1,"deadline":0,"severity":0,"likelihood":0,"workflow":null,"dimension":0,"category_score":null}}]},"